 By Nathan Bomey &middot; Nov 04, 2026 
 
 
 Wednesday ✅. Clouds were lifted last night — both those hanging over the market and the stocks of major cloud computing giants. 

 Today's newsletter is 806 words, a 3-minute read. 

 📈 The dashboard: The S&amp;P 500 closed up 1.6%, its biggest jump since August.

 Treasury yields fell and oil prices retreated. 🔥 Today's stock spotlight: Microsoft (+6.7%), Amazon (+6.1%) and Alphabet (+3.2%) rallied on bets that a Democratic-controlled Congress could slow the AI data center construction boom, easing pressure on the tech giants' massive capital spending plans and strained cash flows.

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Blue wave surfing 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The Democratic Party's blue wave in yesterday's midterm elections washed over Wall Street today, sending stocks sharply higher.

 Here are five key takeaways for investors: 

 🧘 1. The removal of uncertainty is driving market momentum. 

 Traders are happy the midterm elections are over. Now that they know what's next, they're breathing a sigh of relief after a protracted period of uncertainty. "And let's not forget that history shows a big rally post-midterm elections is quite normal and we could be seeing history repeat once again this year," wrote Ryan Detrick, chief market strategist at Carson Group. Investors see gridlock as good — and seem to like the fact that the next two years are likely to be defined by it in Washington. "Expect investigations, not legislation," Jefferies analyst Aniket Shah wrote in a research note. 🥶 2. You're probably having a better day in the markets than Elon Musk. 

 OK, so you're not a trillionaire, but your portfolio probably rose today, while Musk's decidedly did not. In fact, he's not a trillionaire anymore either. SpaceX and Tesla shares fell 3.8% and 3.3% today, respectively, despite the broader market rally. 🤖 3. It was a punch to the gut for earthly data center proponents. 

 Voters embraced the equivalent of data center moratoriums in 43 of 44 spots, according to Brad Carson, president of Americans for Responsible Innovation. Tech analyst and AI bull Dan Ives estimated a 10% reduction in data center construction after the backlash. 🪧 4. It was a win for some unions and a loss for others. 

 Union leaders like the UAW's Shawn Fain got what they wanted: a repudiation of what he calls the "billionaire class." But unions representing electricians, plumbers and pipe fitters won't be happy to see the surging opposition to data centers, which are creating trade jobs. ⛽️ 5. Consumers angry about gas prices might get more relief. 

 The U.S. benchmark price of crude oil plunged 8%, falling below $90 per barrel in a potential prelude to lower prices at the pump. Go deeper 

 
 
